    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p40-q16-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the length of the shortest string that can be wrapped around the circles.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p40-q16-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "2\\pi a+4\\sqrt{ab}-2(a-b)\\cos^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{a-b}{a+b}\\right)",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p40-q16-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the two tangent lengths and the exterior arcs; the inverse cosine is in radians.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $\\theta$ be the angle between the centre line and the larger radius to an upper tangent point, measured in radians. The radii are perpendicular to the tangent. If each straight string segment has length $d$, then\n$$\\begin{align*}d^2&=(a+b)^2-(a-b)^2\\\\d^2&=4ab\\\\d&=2\\sqrt{ab}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe corresponding right triangle gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{a-b}{a+b}\\\\\\theta&=\\cos^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{a-b}{a+b}\\right).\\end{align*}$$\nThe exterior arcs have angles $2\\pi-2\\theta$ and $2\\theta$. Using $L=r\\theta$, the total length is\n$$\\begin{align*}L&=a(2\\pi-2\\theta)+2b\\theta+2d\\\\L&=2\\pi a-2(a-b)\\theta+4\\sqrt{ab}\\\\L&=2\\pi a+4\\sqrt{ab}-2(a-b)\\cos^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{a-b}{a+b}\\right).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q16-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Suppose $a=b$. What does your expression in (a) simplify to?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(2\\pi+4)a$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}L&=2\\pi a+4\\sqrt{a^2}-2(a-a)\\cos^{-1}(0)\\\\L&=(2\\pi+4)a.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q16-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Suppose $a$ is fixed and $b$ approaches $0$. What does your expression in (a) approach?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\pi a$",
          "worked": "As $b\\to0$, $\\sqrt{ab}\\to0$ and $\\cos^{-1}(\\frac{(a-b)}{(a+b)})\\to\\cos^{-1}(1)=0$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}L&\\to2\\pi a+0-2a\\times0\\\\L&\\to2\\pi a.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
